"""Build flow_designer_prompt_v2.6.md from v2.5: results of tests 24 and 25 (2026-11-20), which closed the three weak spots of 2.5. Local files only.
Every replacement must match exactly once."""
import pathlib
R = pathlib.Path(__file__).parent.parent.parent
s = (R / "archive" / "flow_designer_prompt_v2.5.md").read_text()
def rep(old, new):
    global s
    assert s.count(old) == 1, (s.count(old), old[:90]); s = s.replace(old, new)
def repline(start, new):
    global s
    assert s.count("\n" + start) == 1, (s.count("\n" + start), start[:80]); i = s.index("\n" + start) + 1; j = s.index("\n", i); s = s[:i] + new + s[j:]
rep("**Prompt version 2.5** (2026-11-20).", "**Prompt version 2.6** (2026-11-20).")
rep("Append to Flow Variables ran in part. Their rules are in Appendix H.0.", "Append to Flow Variables then ran from its rules, Wait For Message and Wait For Email Reply were resumed by a message and by a reply, and the other Recurrence forms ran (tests 24 and 25). Their rules are in Appendix H.0.")
rep("| Append to Flow Variables `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` in part (Appendix H.0) |", "| Append to Flow Variables `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` (Appendix H.0) |")
rep("| Recurrence (scheduled) `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` (Appendix H.0) |", "| Recurrence (scheduled; not offered in Workflow Studio's trigger list) `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` (Appendix H.0) |")

# ---- waits
repline("  - **Wait For Condition, Wait For Message, Wait For Email Reply:**", '''  - **Wait For Condition, Wait For Message, Wait For Email Reply** (each ran on both paths): set the timeout flag to `1` and give the timeout as a duration (`1970-01-01 00:10:00`). The names differ: `timeout_flag` + `timeout_duration`, `enable_timeout` + `timeout`, `enable_timeout` + `timeout_duration`. Output `state` is `0` when the wait was satisfied and `1` on timeout (Wait For Message has no `state`; on timeout its `payload` is empty).
    - **Wait For Message** resumes when a server script calls `sn_fd.FlowAPI.sendMessage('<sys_id of the waiting run in sys_flow_context>', '<message>', '<payload>')` with the same message text. The payload arrives in `{{<ui_id>.payload}}`. In test 24 a second flow found the waiting run with Look Up Record on `sys_flow_context` (`name=<flow name>^state=WAITING`) and made the call from a Script step of a custom action. Something outside the flow must make that call; say so in `CONFIGURATION_NOTES`.
    - **Wait For Email Reply** takes the outbound email in `record`: the `email` output of an earlier Send Email, `{{<send email ui_id>.email}}`. It resumes when the instance processes a reply to that email (matched by the watermark line `Ref:MSG…` that Send Email adds when `watermark_email` is `1`). Then `state` is `0` and `{{<ui_id>.email_reply}}` is the reply's `sys_email` record (`{{<ui_id>.email_reply.subject}}` resolved).''')

# ---- Append to Flow Variables
repline("- **Append to Flow Variables** `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` in part.", '''- **Append to Flow Variables** `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` (test 24, written from the rules below). It adds elements to a flow variable that is an array of objects. Four parts:
  1. **The variable.** A `sys_hub_flow_variable` row: `element` and `label` of the variable, `internal_type` `string`, `max_length` `65000`, `mandatory` `false`, `order` `1`, `model` and `model_id` = the flow, `model_table` `sys_hub_flow`, `name` `var__m_sys_hub_flow_variable_<flow sys_id>`, and `attributes` = `co_type_name=FD<variable sys_id>,element_mapping_provider=com.glide.flow_design.action.data.FlowDesignVariableMapper,uiType=array.object,uiTypeLabel=Array.Object,uiUniqueId=<flow sys_id>-<element>,child_maxsize=0,child_hint=,child_label=<label of one element>,child_name=item,child_type=object,child_type_label=Object,child_uiUniqueId=<flow sys_id>-<element>-child,max_rows=,child_defaultValue=`. Add its `sys_documentation` row (`name` as above, `element`, `label`, `plural`, `language` `en`) and, before the variable row, `<sys_hub_flow_variable action="delete_multiple" query="model=<flow sys_id>^sys_idNOT IN<variable sys_id>"/>`. Place these right after `sys_hub_flow`.
  2. **The object definition.** A `sys_complex_object` row named `FD<variable sys_id>`, with `namespace` `FlowDesigner`, `type` `complex_object_collection`, and `serialized_content` = the schema below as one line of JSON. The fields sit under `$COCollectionField[0].item`. `<TYPE FACET>` is the same JSON text in both places; the facet values are JSON written as strings, so their quotes are escaped once more.
     ```json
     {"FlowDesigner:FD<variable sys_id>":{"$COCollectionField":[{"item":{
        "<field>":"String","<field>.$field_facets":{"SimpleMapFacet":"{\\"uiTypeLabel\\":\\"String\\",\\"read_only\\":\\"false\\",\\"uiType\\":\\"string\\",\\"choiceOption\\":\\"\\",\\"default_value\\":\\"\\",\\"hint\\":\\"\\",\\"label\\":\\"<field label>\\",\\"mandatory\\":\\"false\\",\\"order\\":\\"1\\",\\"max_length\\":\\"0\\"}"},
        "<field 2>":"Integer","<field 2>.$field_facets":{"SimpleMapFacet":"{\\"uiTypeLabel\\":\\"Integer\\",\\"read_only\\":\\"false\\",\\"uiType\\":\\"integer\\",\\"choiceOption\\":\\"\\",\\"default_value\\":\\"\\",\\"hint\\":\\"\\",\\"label\\":\\"<field 2 label>\\",\\"mandatory\\":\\"false\\",\\"order\\":\\"2\\",\\"max_length\\":\\"0\\"}"}}}],
       "$COCollectionField.$field_facets":{"SimpleMapFacet":"{\\"up-shift-collection-level\\":\\"true\\"}"},
       "$COCollectionField.$type_facets":{"SimpleMapFacet":"<TYPE FACET>"}},
      "FlowDesigner:FD<variable sys_id>.$type_facets":{"SimpleMapFacet":"<TYPE FACET>"}}
     ```
     `<TYPE FACET>` before escaping: `{"sourceId":"","max_rows":"","choiceOption":"","default_value":"","label":"<variable label>","child_hint":"","child_maxsize":"0","mandatory":"false","child_name":"item","uiUniqueId":"<flow sys_id>-<element>","uiTypeLabel":"Array.Object","co_type_name":"FD<variable sys_id>","child_label":"<label of one element>","child_type_label":"Object","read_only":"false","sourceUiUniqueId":"","sourceType":"","hint":"","uiType":"array.object","child_defaultValue":"","child_type":"object","order":"0","max_length":"65000","child_uiUniqueId":"<flow sys_id>-<element>-child"}`. Tested field types: `String` and `Integer`.
  3. **The Append step.** A logic row with `flow_variables_assigned` = the variable's element. `inputs` and `variables` each hold the same one entry: `{"id":"<variable sys_id>","name":"<element>","value":"<serialized object>","displayValue":"<serialized object>","children":[],"parameter":{},"scriptActive":false}`. The serialized object is `{"version":"1.0","complexObjectSchema":<the schema object of part 2>,"complexObject":{"name$":"FD<variable sys_id>","$COCollectionField":[{"<field>":"Alice","<field 2>":1},{"<field>":"Bob","<field 2>":2}]},"serializationFormat":"JSON"}`, written as one line of JSON text in both `value` and `displayValue`. **`$COCollectionField` inside `complexObject` is always a list**, with one object per element to add; a field value may hold a pill (`"{{Created_1.current.number}}"`). SDK 4.14.2 writes a bare object there for one element, and that flow fails at run time with "Undefined value at path null".
  4. **Reading it.** For Each with `items` = `{{flow_variable.<variable>}}` (the variable's element name); inside the loop the fields are `{{<for each ui_id>.item.<field>}}`. In test 24 two Append steps added three elements, and the loop made one task per element: `TT24A item [Alice] id [1]`, `TT24A item [Bob] id [2]`, `TT24A item [INC0010077] id [3]`.''')

# ---- Recurrence
repline("- **Recurrence** `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` for a fixed interval", '''- **Recurrence** `[RUNTIME_TESTED]` in five forms (`trigger_type` `recurrence`, instance `name` `Recurrence`). **Workflow Studio does not offer this trigger.** Its definition carries the attribute `excludeFromTriggerPicker=true` and the state `draft`, and the designer's Scheduled list shows only Daily, Weekly, Monthly, Repeat and Run Once. A generated flow with it publishes and runs, but a person cannot pick or rebuild it in the designer. **Use Daily, Weekly, Monthly, Repeat or Run Once whenever one of them can express the schedule**, and Recurrence only when none can; say so in `CONFIGURATION_NOTES`.
  - Entries every form has: `recurrence` (`choice`, label `Repeat`), `start_date_and_time` (`glide_date_time`, label `Start date and time`, `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS`, read in the activating user's time zone), `end` = `no_end_date` (`choice`, label `End`), `time_zone` = `floating` (`string`, label `Time zone`).
  - `recurrence` = `once`: nothing more. Ran once at the start time; no further run was scheduled.
  - `recurrence` = `daily`: add `frequency` = `1` (`integer`, label `Every`). Ran at the start time; the next run was scheduled one day later.
  - `recurrence` = `weekly`: add `frequency` = `1` and `days_of_the_week` (`day_of_week`, label `Days of the week`, Monday `1` to Sunday `7`). Ran at the start time on that weekday; next run seven days later.
  - `recurrence` = `monthly`: add `frequency` = `1`, `day_selection` = `fixed_day` (`choice`, label `On`) and `day_of_month` (`integer`, label `Fixed day`). Ran at the start time on that day; next run one month later.
  - `recurrence` = `periodically`: add `repeat` = the interval as a duration (`glide_duration`, label `Every`, `1970-01-01 00:02:00` for two minutes). Ran when it was activated and then on every interval counted from the start time.
  - Not run: `yearly`, a `frequency` above 1, `relative_weekday` ("second Tuesday"), `end` = `after` or `end_date`, business calendars. For those, follow Section 7.8.
  - Publishing writes a `sys_flow_enhanced_timer_trigger` row with these values. Pill `{{Recurrence_1.run_start_date_time}}`.''')
rep("- **Knowledge Management** `[OBSERVED]` (`trigger_type` `knowledge_management`, instance `name` `Knowledge Management`):", "- **Knowledge Management** `[OBSERVED]` (`trigger_type` `knowledge_management`, instance `name` `Knowledge Management`; its definition is in state `draft` on the test instance):")

rep("`COMPLETE`, two loop passes; the element field pills were logged unresolved |", "`COMPLETE`, two loop passes; the element field pills were logged unresolved (a fault of that test file, which pointed the pills at a loop id that no longer existed; see test 24) |")
# ---- Appendix C rows
ROW = "| Recurrence trigger, every two minutes (test 23, flow C) | Activated, then deactivated after two runs | Two incidents `Made by TT23C recurrence`, 34 seconds apart (one at activation, one on the interval) |"
rep(ROW, ROW + '''
| Array-of-objects flow variable, two Append to Flow Variables steps (two elements, then one with a pill), For Each reading the element fields; all written from the rules (test 24, flow A) | One incident | Three tasks: `TT24A item [Alice] id [1]`, `TT24A item [Bob] id [2]`, `TT24A item [INC0010077] id [3]` |
| Wait For Message resumed by `sn_fd.FlowAPI.sendMessage` from a second flow's custom action (test 24, flows B and C) | Two incidents | `TT24B resumed, payload [hello from INC0010080]`; sender `TT24C [sent TT24MSG to <context sys_id>]` |
| Wait For Email Reply resumed by a reply (test 24, flow D) | One incident, then a reply email carrying the watermark | `TT24D reply state [0] reply [Re: TT24D out INC0010079]` |
| Recurrence trigger, forms `once`, `daily`, `weekly`, `monthly` (test 25) | Start time set five minutes ahead | Four executions at 17:14:01 UTC, the minute set (9:14 AM Pacific). Next runs scheduled for the next day, seven days later and one month later; none for `once` |''')
rep("\nTest index: `verification/TESTS.md`.",
    "| **2.6** | 2026-11-20 | `flow_designer_prompt_v2.6.md` | Tests 24 and 25 closed the three weak spots of 2.5. **Append to Flow Variables** now has complete rules (variable row, object definition, serialized value, reading in For Each) and ran from them; the unresolved field pills of test 22 were a fault in that test file, not in the platform. **Wait For Message** and **Wait For Email Reply** ran on their success paths. **Recurrence** ran in five forms; it is not offered in Workflow Studio's trigger list, so the prompt prefers the five listed schedule triggers. |\n\nTest index: `verification/TESTS.md`.")
(R / "archive" / "flow_designer_prompt_v2.6.md").write_text(s)
print("written", len(s), "chars,", s.count("\n") + 1, "lines")
